% An IF or COND whose every result is a text literal keeps SEL's
% identity in SQL (SEL-0057). SQL's CASE hands back the literal it chose,
% byte for byte -- verified on PostgreSQL 17 and MariaDB 11.8 with 'a'/'A' and
% 'a'/'a ', in memory after a split and under SQL's own DISTINCT (which the
% translator collates exactly). So DEDUPE and BUCKET over it are exact, and
% the planner's identity barrier lets a MAP that computes one end a prefix.
%
% What stays refused: a numeric result, which a CASE can re-spell (MariaDB
% answers 1.0 for IF(c, 1, 1.0)'s 1, and a DEDUPE then loses a row); a column
% or any computed result, whose spelling the literal rule says nothing about;
% results of different kinds, which the translator refuses outright. CANON is
% how a rule makes a numeric result provable.

### name: textif.postgresql.dedupe
--- note
Was E_SQL_SHAPE: "grouping depends on a computed projection without
identity preservation".
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "fields": {"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}, "T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> MAP(RECORD("s", IF(_["n"] > 1, "a", "b"))) .> DEDUPE()
--- expect
SELECT DISTINCT CAST(CASE WHEN ("n" > 1) THEN 'a' ELSE 'b' END AS TEXT) COLLATE "C" AS "s" FROM "r"
===

### name: textif.postgresql.cond
--- dialect
postgresql
--- bindings
{"R": {"kind": "relation", "from": "r", "fields": {"N": {"kind": "column", "column": "n", "type": "NUM"}, "T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- as
statement
--- source
R .> MAP(RECORD("s", COND(_["n"] > 1, "a", _["n"] > 0, "b", "c"))) .> DEDUPE()
--- expect
SELECT DISTINCT CAST(CASE WHEN ("n" > 1) THEN 'a' WHEN ("n" > 0) THEN 'b' ELSE 'c' END AS TEXT) COLLATE "C" AS "s" FROM "r"
===

